To minimize the length of a string by repeatedly deleting the closest occurrences of the same character to the left and right of a chosen index, the key insight is that it is possible to remove all but one occurrence of each distinct character in the string. This is because, for any character that appears multiple times, we can iteratively remove the outer occurrences by choosing an index that has both a left and right occurrence of the same character. This process can be repeated until only one occurrence of each character remains. Therefore, the minimal length of the string is simply the number of distinct characters in the original string.

Here is the reference code that implements this solution:

```python
class Solution:
    def minimizedStringLength(self, s: str) -> int:
        return len(set(s))
```

This code works by converting the string into a set of characters, which automatically removes any duplicate characters, and then returning the length of this set. This length represents the number of distinct characters in the string, which is the minimal possible length after performing the specified operations.
